Doug Mallory wrote:
> 
> I have a weird problem on a site, and can't figure out how this is
> happening. During a day, several files are created, with print data in them,
> without being in a directory. After a ana/disk/repair I get these files in
> the syslost directory, all names   .dat;*.
> How can this happen without something complaining, and how can these files
> even get created this way, and of course, how do I fix it?
> 
> Thanks for any help... Doug.

You can create 'temporary' files with RMS by setting the FAB$M_TMP bit
in the FAB$L_FOP options longword of the FAB or by specifying FILE
DIRECTORY_ENTRY NO in a .FDL file. This causes the file to be created,
but with no directory entry. You can still access the file by its FID
(file identification).

This option is mostly used when a device is set to 'spooled'; copying
data to the device results in the creation of a print job. Since the
file should remain invisible during print, it is created as temporary
file.

The file is deleted by the queue manager when the print job completes.
If, however, for some reason, this is not possible (queue is stopped or
stalled), the file still continues to exist, but also still remains
invisible.

ANALYZE/DISK detects files without directory entry and places them in
the [SYSLOST] directory.

Since you wrote that the files have print data, i'd assume the above
scenario. Check all spooled devices and all queues they point to. Type
SHOW QUEUE/ALL/FULL to see whether incopleted print jobs exist.

Greetings, Ferry

