Ben,

In much the same vein as other posts, I have to question the sanity of
this project.  Another one falls to the whims of WinTel and BG.

Gripes aside, I vaguely remember seeing a post out here in the past 2
months or so that discussed a little company that specialized in reading
multiple tape formats on NT and that they might also have something to
read Files 11 ODS Level 2 formatted storage media on NT.  I'm doing a
bad thing here - just repeating what I kinda remember hearing/reading
but for what its worth try DejaNews and see of you can dig it up.

On another note, what's the cost to replace the media?  Are the disks
very expensive?  Would it be possible to simply attach a client
workstation thad NT and PathWorks access and simply use the client
machine to copy the data over to the NT server?

Another item to consider is the age of the optical system, the storage
capacity of the media and the drivers and the performance of the library
system.  Based on the price/performance characteristics of modern 9.1GB
SCSI drives you may be better suited by creating a RAID set of some
level out of 3 to 8 of these huge SCSI drives and just use them in place
of the optical drives.  Ideally, a DEC SWXCR or other similar PCI bus
RAID controller with a StorageWorks shelf loaded with RZ40 disk drives
would make a great high capacity moderate performance archive pool that
could compete very well with the older optical systems.

Combine this with a very fast high density DLT tape backup drive and
you're ready to rock-n-roll.

I have run across several imaging database vendors who are now dumping
the optical technology in favor of the high capacity disk drives in
their new systems that they are setting up.


Chuck
