When I converted from my MicroVAX 3100 Pathworks server to an Alpha 3000/400
about 5 years ago, I just unplugged all my Pathworks user data SCSI Hard
drives from the VAX into the Alpha and rebooted. Everything ran fine - just
10 times faster.

Its also possible to use a Windows NT 4.0 Server as a gateway to all the
Pathworks information (it needs a Pathworks license to do this - but you
already have those if you have Pathworks now). I do this at my location.
You're just limited to 20 some drive letters per NT server for mapping
purposes to use for creating shares on the NT. Then everybody sees NT and
only you, the poor system manager, has to know what's really happening.

Why work harder than you have to? I know you'll spend your time keeping NT
functional once it comes through the door. Why mess with something that's
working if you don't have to? If the users (management?) want to see NT, its
possible to layer it on top of your other systems for them and keep you from
having to perform migrations (and lots of work) that doesn't add much value.

